<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=mudbrick">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mudbrick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 9000 BCE</span> · Also known as Mud-brick, Adobe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A mudbrick is an air-dried, unfired brick of mud mixed with a binder such as straw or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> husks, first known from the dwellings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>, dated to 9000 BCE. Sun-dried mudbricks were the most common construction material in pharaonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and people at Mehrgarh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> lived in mudbrick houses between 7000 and 3300 BCE. From around 5000–4000 BCE they evolved into fired bricks, but they remain standard in vernacular building in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and western Asia. The Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> is the world's largest mudbrick structure.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mudbrick.jpg" alt="Mudbrick" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mud-brick_stamped_with_seal_impression_of_raised_relief_of_the_Treasury_of_the_Vizier._From_Lahun,_Fayum,_Egypt._12th_Dynasty._The_Petrie_Museum_of_Egyptian_Archaeology,_London.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
